```python
extremes(data, variable, year=None, *, n=5, title=None, ref=None)
```

The units with the highest and the lowest values of a variable in one year.

## Parameters {.doc-section .doc-section-parameters}

| Name     | Type        | Description                                                                 | Default    |
|----------|-------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|------------|
| data     |             | A dataset id, or a panel with ``geo_id`` (and ``year``), e.g. from ``load`` | _required_ |
| variable | str         | The numeric column to rank                                                  | _required_ |
| year     |             | The year to rank (default: the last with data)                              | `None`     |
| n        | int         | How many units to show at each end                                          | `5`        |
| title    | str \| None | The figure and table title                                                  | `None`     |

## Returns {.doc-section .doc-section-returns}

| Name   | Type           | Description                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        |
|--------|----------------|--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|        | ExtremesResult | A result with ``.df`` (the ``n`` highest and the ``n`` lowest units: ``geo_id``, ``name``, the group columns of the table, the variable, ``rank`` with 1 for the highest, and ``end``: ``"highest"`` or ``"lowest"``), ``.fig`` (horizontal bars, the two ends in different colours), ``.gt``, ``.highest``, ``.lowest``, ``.median``, ``.n_units`` (units with data), ``.year``, ``.notes`` and ``.interpret()``. |

## Example {.doc-section .doc-section-example}

```python
!pip install -q geo4bolivia
import geo4bolivia as geo

mpi = geo.load("sdg01", variables="sdg1_2_mpi", groups=["department"])
res = geo.extremes(mpi, "sdg1_2_mpi", year=2024, n=5)
print(res.interpret())
res.fig
```

